(Discrepancy 5 — the "bare literal in a slot" diagnostic (FMT-18,
 LANGUAGE.md:3329-3332) is anchored at the first TEXTUAL occurrence of the
 slot in the file, comments included, not at the code that caused it. Here
 the only real slot is on line 2, but the caret lands on line 1, inside a
 comment. Comments are otherwise inert: this same comment WITHOUT line 2
 compiles clean, so the comment is not what is being compiled — only what
 is being pointed at.
 Pro-compiler reading: the diagnostic carries the slot's identity rather
 than its position, and the renderer recovers a position by searching the
 source for the slot text; on a file where the slot appears once, which is
 every file the compiler's own tests would have, the recovered position is
 right. It is a source-span recovery weakness in the error renderer, not a
 wrong verdict — the program really is invalid and really is refused.
 Cosmetic, but it will mislead anyone debugging a generated program, and
 generated programs carry ledger IDs and claim text in their comments.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox probe.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   error: Unknown variable: 255
   (a compile error, but reported at line 1 — inside the comment — rather
    than at line 2, which is the statement that is actually invalid)
)
(this comment mentions {255:x} and is where the caret lands)
Print "{255:x}".
